{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition","id":685695,"title":"Silver 2nd edition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-02T07:14:55-07:00","id":685695,"title":"Silver 2nd edition","content":"I remember some rules from the 1st edition about possible -1 or 2 for shooting silver bullets.<br /> Any rules about silver weapons for the 2nd edition ?<br /> I'm talking about rules about shooting and ranged weapons. Silver is not the best metal to forge bullets.<br /> the book also say that a weapon should be made at least of 80% silver to inflict aggravated damages.<br /> <br /> Also, anyone who know something about silver used for bullets in real shooting? Are there any consquences for the weapon, realistically?<br /> I'm totally about swordfight and close combat. I barely now how to shoot with a shotgun.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\">-'' </span>We are the unsullied.<br /> We are the inheritors.<br /> We are the Pure <span style=\"color:#800000\">''-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3773-helur","name":"Helur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3773_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"352"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-02T07:37:46-07:00","id":685748,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Helur</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition#post685695\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remember some rules from the 1st edition about possible -1 or 2 for shooting silver bullets.<br /> Any rules about silver weapons for the 2nd edition ?<br /> I'm talking about rules about shooting and ranged weapons. Silver is not the best metal to forge bullets.<br /> the book also say that a weapon should be made at least of 80% silver to inflict aggravated damages.<br /> <br /> Also, anyone who know something about silver used for bullets in real shooting? Are there any consquences for the weapon, realistically?<br /> I'm totally about swordfight and close combat. I barely now how to shoot with a shotgun.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div> The \u200b2e rules are that a silver item not designed as a weapon counts as improvised, but otherwise there are no penalties.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-02T12:00:20-07:00","id":686031,"title":"","content":"Silver is harder than lead so there would be increased wear and tear on the gun, but probably not enough to be worth dealing with in a game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-02T13:45:16-07:00","id":686135,"title":"","content":"I would still lower their damage rating.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-06T21:20:28-08:00","id":802646,"title":"","content":"2e states that they are treated as improvised but that gives a -1 on dice pool, which <i>could</i> affect damage but that would also influence the chance to hit and, while that was &quot;ok&quot; in 1e, now in 2e they're separating damage rating from dice pool to hit.<br /> <br /> It seems troublesome to constantly say &quot;composed of 80% or more of silver&quot; like the book says, so I'll just say &quot;pure silver&quot; and by &quot;pure&quot; I mean 80% or more, yeah.<br /> <br /> I don't agree with that rule because it would make firearms with silver bullets breakable upon little use and also give that -1 penalty for usage, which makes no sense for firearms. Here is what I'd use:<br /> <br /> <b>For bullets or arrows/bolts:</b> one of the major factors when considering damage from projectiles is their &quot;mommentum&quot; - which is their consistency of &quot;keep going&quot; after impact. So the easier a projecticle breaks or smashes itself upon impact, the less impact and penetration it'll have on whatever it is colliding. Silver is less resistant than steel (and other carbon-mixed metals they use for bullets) so bullets and arrow/bolts should do less damage. Giving -1L for this system would be enough to show that. The weapon should otherwise be used as normal.<br /> <br /> <b>For melee and thrown weapons:</b> Silver is densier than steel, including stainless steel, about 33% (1/3) more and thus is notably heavier for melee weapons of the same design. Its not heavier enough to benefit from +1 damage, if you consider how large are the weight difference between weapons of different damage ratings. It is, though, heavier enough to consider the -1 penalty for usage (melee and throwing) and definetly use the breakeage rules for improvised weapons. Its not enough heavier to increase their strenght requirment but its heavy for its size and design, hence the improvised penalty. As for damage, you could say the extra weight compensates for the flaws mentioned above of silver being used for weapons. keeping their damage intact or you could just give -1L if you feel they should do less damage (I would keep their damage intact). For all effects, &quot;pure silver&quot; weapons should also have -1 durability compared to their &quot;intended&quot; weapons, assuming they are fine-crafted. This makes them easy to be &quot;sundered&quot; if attacked and specialy easier to break upon constant use in a combat, which is what <i>should</i> happen.<br /> <br /> As for costs... A &quot;pure&quot; silver melee or thrown would cost about 5 times more than a normal fine-crafted weapon. So considering the resources/avaliability rules, +1 dot on their cost would be exactly how much more they should cost. As for bullets... I'd say <i>one</i> full pistol clip could cost 2 dots while 12 revolver bullets or six shotgun rounds could have the same cost rating.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10798,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10798-kitsune-sensei","user":"Kitsune Sensei","date":". 01-07-2016, 12:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10798-kitsune-sensei","name":"Kitsune Sensei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10798_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-06T23:31:35-08:00","id":802814,"title":"","content":"I've been wondering how I should go about this, I haven't change rules for silver in my game, but your reply makes a lot of sense Kitsune. Think I'm going to use those rules from now on.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8177-heroofthemists","name":"Heroofthemists","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"95"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T06:43:48-08:00","id":803043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kitsune Sensei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=802646#post802646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2e states that they are treated as improvised but that gives a -1 on dice pool, which <i>could</i> affect damage but that would also influence the chance to hit and, while that was &quot;ok&quot; in 1e, now in 2e they're separating damage rating from dice pool to hit.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're only improvised if they're not designed for combat. E.g. a decorative sword.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kitsune Sensei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=802646#post802646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't agree with that rule because it would make firearms with silver bullets breakable upon little use and also give that -1 penalty for usage, which makes no sense for firearms. Here is what I'd use:<br /> <br /> <b>For bullets or arrows/bolts:</b> one of the major factors when considering damage from projectiles is their &quot;mommentum&quot; - which is their consistency of &quot;keep going&quot; after impact. So the easier a projecticle breaks or smashes itself upon impact, the less impact and penetration it'll have on whatever it is colliding. Silver is less resistant than steel (and other carbon-mixed metals they use for bullets) so bullets and arrow/bolts should do less damage. Giving -1L for this system would be enough to show that. The weapon should otherwise be used as normal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hollow-point rounds are specifically designed to break apart, to cause more damage to soft tissues. They are therefore less effective against armored targets. This directly opposes what you are saying.<br /> <br /> Secondly, modern bullets are still made largely of lead. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet#Materials\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet#Materials</a><br /> Solid silver (near room temperature) is ~10g/cm^3 and lead ~11g/cm^3. Not a significant difference.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/1280px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\"> Silver - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/1280px-Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\"> Lead - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kitsune Sensei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=802646#post802646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>For melee and thrown weapons:</b> Silver is densier than steel, including stainless steel, about 33% (1/3) more and thus is notably heavier for melee weapons of the same design. Its not heavier enough to benefit from +1 damage, if you consider how large are the weight difference between weapons of different damage ratings. It is, though, heavier enough to consider the -1 penalty for usage (melee and throwing) and definetly use the breakeage rules for improvised weapons. Its not enough heavier to increase their strenght requirment but its heavy for its size and design, hence the improvised penalty. As for damage, you could say the extra weight compensates for the flaws mentioned above of silver being used for weapons. keeping their damage intact or you could just give -1L if you feel they should do less damage (I would keep their damage intact). For all effects, &quot;pure silver&quot; weapons should also have -1 durability compared to their &quot;intended&quot; weapons, assuming they are fine-crafted. This makes them easy to be &quot;sundered&quot; if attacked and specialy easier to break upon constant use in a combat, which is what <i>should</i> happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Analyzing steels vs. silver for melee weapons is a lot harder. I wouldn't make an entire weapon out of silver, it WOULD be a lot heavier. But also horribly wasteful. Assuming that the contact with silver is what causes harm, then you can coat your steel weapon in silver or a silver alloy (80%!) and hone it.<br /> I don't have any information about how well this would work, but it would largely be similar in its handling.<br /> <br /> Blundgeoning weapons would probably suffer little to no change at all. A decent coating of silver would likely not be scraped away quickly and minimally impact handling.<br /> <br /> If you're not coating or plating, but instead making the entire weapon of silver... that's harder. I don't know why Kitsune is so confident that 33% heavier weapons don't qualify for +1L but do for -1 to hit. There aren't any guideline about this.<br /> <br /> In conclusion:<br /> Use the rules presented in the book. Modify them slightly if you feel that it will serve the narrative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5292,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5292-flailbot","user":"FlailBot","date":". 01-07-2016, 09:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5292-flailbot","name":"FlailBot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T12:03:02-08:00","id":803248,"title":"","content":"There are a lot of issues with silver bullets. First of all, they're incredibly hard to actually make because the melting point for silver is so high. Secondly, it tends to warp when it cools, so the bullets end up irregularly shaped and too small. The end result is that they are expensive, hard to make, and result in a technically functional but very inaccurate bullet, which will presumably be rougher on the gun as well. I'd say the -1 improvised weapon penalty is actually a pretty good way to model the decreased accuracy. WoD doesn't model risk of guns jamming without additional rules hacks, so I would just ignore that part. I'd probably also rule that since they're not commercially available, you need a Contact or Ally to buy them, and I'd slap a small penalty on Crafts rolls to make them yourself, to model the difficulty of actually making them.<br /> <br /> Also, @FailBot, the rules specifically state that silver coating or plating only works for the first hit. The 80% thing is about the composition of the object, not the purity of the silver.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 01-07-2016, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T12:19:38-08:00","id":803257,"title":"","content":"I dunno why people are looking at silver blades and not, you know, silver hammers or the like. Bludgeoning a werewolf with silver would still deal Aggravated, after all.<br /> <br /> So silver brass knuckles would let you deal Aggravated to werewolves with your Brawl attacks, wouldn't be terribly less durable, and I sincerely doubt you'd suffer any improvised penalties for them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T12:20:10-08:00","id":803259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FlailBot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=803043#post803043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They're only improvised if they're not designed for combat. E.g. a decorative sword.<br /> <br /> Hollow-point rounds are specifically designed to break apart, to cause more damage to soft tissues. They are therefore less effective against armored targets. This directly opposes what you are saying.<br /> <br /> Secondly, modern bullets are still made largely of lead. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet#Materials\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet#Materials</a><br /> Solid silver (near room temperature) is ~10g/cm^3 and lead ~11g/cm^3. Not a significant difference.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/1280px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\"> Silver - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/1280px-Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\"> Lead - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Analyzing steels vs. silver for melee weapons is a lot harder. I wouldn't make an entire weapon out of silver, it WOULD be a lot heavier. But also horribly wasteful. Assuming that the contact with silver is what causes harm, then you can coat your steel weapon in silver or a silver alloy (80%!) and hone it.<br /> I don't have any information about how well this would work, but it would largely be similar in its handling.<br /> <br /> Blundgeoning weapons would probably suffer little to no change at all. A decent coating of silver would likely not be scraped away quickly and minimally impact handling.<br /> <br /> If you're not coating or plating, but instead making the entire weapon of silver... that's harder. I don't know why Kitsune is so confident that 33% heavier weapons don't qualify for +1L but do for -1 to hit. There aren't any guideline about this.<br /> <br /> In conclusion:<br /> Use the rules presented in the book. Modify them slightly if you feel that it will serve the narrative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hollow-point is designed to scatter in the victim's body because they're literaly cut on design to shatter in fragments.Silver knead notably easier than lead even though they have similar density. With less mommentum, there is less final impact and penetration and, because it is not cut as hollow-point, there is no extra damage. Even a hollow-point made of &quot;pure&quot; silver would have less penetration factor on its fragments for the same reasons.<br /> <br /> Because of mythical folklore, scientists and weaponsmiths were asked a lot about the effectiveness of silver bullets used in firearms and they always list the inconveniences of silver composition being used as material. Accuracy also drops because of the weight difference but it only happens on medium to long ranges (100 yards and more).<br /> <br /> For melee weapons, its worse because the metal bends on blades if it hits hard surface or is parried by a steel weapon. It also can't hold a good edge because its low hardiness. Bludgeoning weapons would kneed upon use. It's not expected to happen on first hit, so the breaking rules makes sense. Maybe I exagerated on suggesting to reduce its durabiliy by one, but I'm still not sure because of the hardiness thing.<br /> <br /> If you just go by the book, silver weapons (that are crafted to be weapons), as you said, are just like a weapon in all its purpose except causing aggravated damage to werewolves. If you feel reality shouldn't hold back in the uratha's bane for a little bit (just -1L or the improvised condition), then going by the book fits perfectly for your narrative. Was just laying some suggestions for whomever felt like silver weapons should show less effectiveness in their game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10798-kitsune-sensei","name":"Kitsune Sensei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10798_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T01:47:50-08:00","id":803622,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=803248#post803248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are a lot of issues with silver bullets. First of all, they're incredibly hard to actually make because the melting point for silver is so high. Secondly, it tends to warp when it cools, so the bullets end up irregularly shaped and too small. The end result is that they are expensive, hard to make, and result in a technically functional but very inaccurate bullet, which will presumably be rougher on the gun as well. I'd say the -1 improvised weapon penalty is actually a pretty good way to model the decreased accuracy. WoD doesn't model risk of guns jamming without additional rules hacks, so I would just ignore that part. I'd probably also rule that since they're not commercially available, you need a Contact or Ally to buy them, and I'd slap a small penalty on Crafts rolls to make them yourself, to model the difficulty of actually making them.<br /> <br /> Also, @FailBot, the rules specifically state that silver coating or plating only works for the first hit. The 80% thing is about the composition of the object, not the purity of the silver.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, thanks. I didn't know the plating thing.<br /> <br /> Regarding manufacture, I think that some of these issues would be avoided by improved expertise. Early lead ball rounds suffered from quality issues as well. I would say that you could represent the difficulty of manufacture by making the test harder (as you suggested) (or failure DOES impose a penalty to use). Matter of production scale, I suppose.<br /> <br /> Most bullets are cased in a non-lead metal, to protect the lead core and the barrel from one another. I would hope the same for silver bullets, so concerns about damaging the barrel or inaccuracy due to deformation are overstated, imo.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kitsune Sensei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=803259#post803259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hollow-point is designed to scatter in the victim's body because they're literaly cut on design to shatter in fragments.Silver knead notably easier than lead even though they have similar density. With less mommentum, there is less final impact and penetration and, because it is not cut as hollow-point, there is no extra damage. Even a hollow-point made of &quot;pure&quot; silver would have less penetration factor on its fragments for the same reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Firstly, sorry if I came off as a bit stand-offish in the previous post.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the difference in the momentum of a silver bullet and lead bullet would be less than 10%. I do not feel that it very significant. One consideration might be the hardness of silver, but lead is also very soft, so I don't see this being distinctly different either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kitsune Sensei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/685695-silver-2nd-edition?p=803259#post803259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because of mythical folklore, scientists and weaponsmiths were asked a lot about the effectiveness of silver bullets used in firearms and they always list the inconveniences of silver composition being used as material. Accuracy also drops because of the weight difference but it only happens on medium to long ranges (100 yards and more).<br /> <br /> For melee weapons, its worse because the metal bends on blades if it hits hard surface or is parried by a steel weapon. It also can't hold a good edge because its low hardiness. Bludgeoning weapons would kneed upon use. It's not expected to happen on first hit, so the breaking rules makes sense. Maybe I exagerated on suggesting to reduce its durabiliy by one, but I'm still not sure because of the hardiness thing.<br /> <br /> If you just go by the book, silver weapons (that are crafted to be weapons), as you said, are just like a weapon in all its purpose except causing aggravated damage to werewolves. If you feel reality shouldn't hold back in the uratha's bane for a little bit (just -1L or the improvised condition), then going by the book fits perfectly for your narrative. Was just laying some suggestions for whomever felt like silver weapons should show less effectiveness in their game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think at the end of the day, I would see how much my players were willing to invest in the procurement and penalize them based on that. If they're just running to the store and grabbing decorative silver plated swords, then they're taking the -1 and it's only good for 1 hit. If they're buying bullets on the cheap from a crazed gunsmith, then maybe a -1 (unless they spend some time, therefore effort/resources on practicing) and dramatic failures jam the gun.<br /> <br /> If they're really investing though, I'd be happy to give them basically a normal weapon + silver effects. They'll still have to deal with the narrative consequences of going for the nuclear option though!! :-D","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5292-flailbot","name":"FlailBot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T03:13:34-08:00","id":803642,"title":"","content":"There's two schools of thought at work here. One that's concerned about something like game balance and anothet that wants to simulate stuff that's basically above the system's capabilities.<br /> <br /> You won't catch me studying ballistics in preparation for my game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}